===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} An [[edible]] [[species]] of [[tuna]], {{taxfmt|Thunnus albacares|species}}, found especially in [[tropical]] seas.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=Gary T. Sakagawa|chapter=Western Pacific '''Yellowfin Tuna''' Research Group Findings on Tuna Fisheries Interactions|editors=Richard S. Shomura; Jacek Majkowski; Robert F. Harman|title=Status of Interactions of Pacific Tuna Fisheries in 1995|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=NBGoaAViKGAC&pg=PA310&dq=%22yellowfin+tuna%22%7C%22yellowfin+tunas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=wp7fUqPSEsfNlAXk94HoBA&ved=0CGIQ6AEwBg#v=onepage&q=%22yellowfin%20tuna%22%7C%22yellowfin%20tunas%22&f=false|page=310
|passage=The experiment in the first period targeted skipjack tuna and the second, '''yellowfin tuna'''.}}
#* '''2009''', Kurt M. Schaefer, Daniel W. Fuller, Barbara A. Block, ''Vertical Movements and Habitat Utilization of Skipjack (''Katsuwonis pelamis''), Yellowfin ({{taxfmt|Thunnus albacares|species}}), and Bigeye ({{taxlink|Thunnus obesus|species}}) Tunas in the Equatorial Eastern Pacific Ocean, Ascertained through Archival Tag Data'',
#*: During the daytime, skipjack and '''yellowfin tunas''' occasionally exhibited repetitive bounce-dive foraging behavior well below the thermocline to depths of the deep scattering layer, between 225 and 400 m.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Yasushi Mitsunaga; Ricardo Barbaran; C. Endo; Kazuhiko Anraku|chapter=Swimming Behavior of Juvenile '''Yellowfin Tuna''' ({{taxfmt|Thunnus albacares|species}}) Around Fish Aggragate{{sic}} Devices (F.A.D.S.) in the Philippines|editor=Hubert J. Ceccaldi|title=Global Change: Mankind-Marine Environment Interactions|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=eLI2jBSjL78C&pg=PA123&dq=%22yellowfin+tuna%22%7C%22yellowfin+tunas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=wp7fUqPSEsfNlAXk94HoBA&ved=0CHoQ6AEwCw#v=onepage&q=%22yellowfin%20tuna%22%7C%22yellowfin%20tunas%22&f=false|page=123
|passage=Ohta and Kakuma (2005) reported that adult '''yellowfin tuna''' showed a similar diurnal vertical swimming pattern and dived to depths over 200 m.}}

